WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:01.410
- Here's a legal pro tip.

00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:02.940
If you admit to opposing counsel

00:00:02.940 --> 00:00:04.950
that he's having a Perry Mason moment

00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:06.060
while cross-examining you...

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:07.890
- If this is your Perry Mason moment-

00:00:07.890 --> 00:00:09.900
- Well, you're probably screwed,

00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:12.690
and that is exactly what
happened to Alex Jones.

00:00:12.690 --> 00:00:15.540
Alex Jones got so skewered
on cross-examination

00:00:15.540 --> 00:00:17.070
that the writers of "Law &amp; Order" said

00:00:17.070 --> 00:00:19.260
it was too unrealistic for the show.

00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:20.760
Now, to say that this defamation trial

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:23.610
by some of the Sandy Hook
families against Alex Jones

00:00:23.610 --> 00:00:26.820
is going poorly for Jones
is quite the understatement.

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:28.830
Now, to recall how we got here,

00:00:28.830 --> 00:00:31.110
Jones is already in hot water.

00:00:31.110 --> 00:00:33.750
This trial is only over damages,

00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:37.110
how much Jones is going to
have to pay these families,

00:00:37.110 --> 00:00:38.790
because he and his lawyers engaged

00:00:38.790 --> 00:00:42.810
in so many discovery abuses
that the judge sanctioned him,

00:00:42.810 --> 00:00:44.700
gave him effectively terminating sanctions

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:46.980
where he had to concede liability.

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:48.150
Jones lost the case,

00:00:48.150 --> 00:00:51.030
and the Sandy Hook
plaintiffs won by default.

00:00:51.030 --> 00:00:53.940
That's how abusive they
were during discovery.

00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:55.770
But that takes us to the viral video

00:00:55.770 --> 00:00:57.330
that you might have seen clips of

00:00:57.330 --> 00:00:59.490
where the plaintiffs'
attorney, Mark Bankston,

00:00:59.490 --> 00:01:02.940
absolutely destroys Alex Jones on cross

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:06.960
after just setting him
up for a gigantic fall.

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:08.520
So let's take a look.

00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:09.870
- [Mark] One of the things
you talked about yesterday

00:01:09.870 --> 00:01:11.770
is you complied with discovery, right?

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:15.480
You said that on the witness stand.

00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:17.280
- That's one of the things I talked about.

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:20.280
- I mean, Jones did not
actually comply with discovery,

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:22.740
but I guess now they're saying
after all the sanctions,

00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:26.490
they have upheld all of their obligations.

00:01:26.490 --> 00:01:28.050
- [Mark] One of the things
that you were ordered

00:01:28.050 --> 00:01:31.110
to do in this lawsuit, you were ordered

00:01:31.110 --> 00:01:34.830
to turn over any text messages
mentioning Sandy Hook.

00:01:34.830 --> 00:01:36.030
Right?
- Yes.

00:01:36.030 --> 00:01:39.120
- Yeah, so probably the very first request

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:42.060
that the plaintiffs would've
made is any documents

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:43.890
that mention the phrase Sandy Hook

00:01:43.890 --> 00:01:47.850
or refer to the events of Sandy
Hook that Jones possesses.

00:01:47.850 --> 00:01:49.560
This is discovery 101.

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:51.450
These are the kind of
requests you're going to make.

00:01:51.450 --> 00:01:53.450
- [Mark] And you didn't have any, right?

00:01:54.450 --> 00:01:55.550
- Not that we could find.

00:01:55.550 --> 00:01:58.380
- Now, that's a bit of a cop-out by Jones.

00:01:58.380 --> 00:02:00.120
He's saying, "You know,
I might've had them,

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:01.620
but we couldn't find them."

00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:03.810
That doesn't get out of his obligations

00:02:03.810 --> 00:02:06.150
because you have to do a reasonable search

00:02:06.150 --> 00:02:07.500
and your lawyers have to make sure

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:08.520
that you do a reasonable search

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:10.980
to find responsive documents.

00:02:10.980 --> 00:02:14.340
- [Mark] And you, in fact,
told me in your testimony,

00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:16.470
sworn testimony, before
coming to this courtroom.

00:02:16.470 --> 00:02:17.941
You searched, right?

00:02:17.941 --> 00:02:18.867
- I did.
- Okay.

00:02:18.867 --> 00:02:20.857
- All right, so Bankston has set this up.

00:02:20.857 --> 00:02:22.740
"You've testified that you didn't have

00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:25.560
any Sandy Hook-related texts and that,

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:28.620
of course, you did a search for them."

00:02:28.620 --> 00:02:31.470
It's all downhill from here for Jones.

00:02:31.470 --> 00:02:32.640
- [Mark] Do you remember...

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:35.310
Were you here for Mr. Shroyer's testimony?

00:02:35.310 --> 00:02:36.143
- Yes.
- Okay.

00:02:36.143 --> 00:02:39.300
Do you remember when Owen
said, "The company has learned

00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:41.340
from its mistakes about Sandy Hook?"

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:42.173
Do you remember that?

00:02:42.173 --> 00:02:43.927
- Oh. I did hear him say that.

00:02:43.927 --> 00:02:45.510
- [Mark] Do you agree with that?

00:02:45.510 --> 00:02:47.100
- Yeah, we certainly
learned from our mistakes.

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:48.400
Got a lot better.
- Okay.

00:02:49.558 --> 00:02:51.120
- Mr. Jones, I'd like to
show you what's been marked

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:52.593
as plaintiff's exhibit 130.

00:02:56.070 --> 00:02:57.370
You've got it upside down.

00:02:58.860 --> 00:03:01.673
That's text messages between
you and Paul Watson, isn't it?

00:03:02.550 --> 00:03:03.383
- Yes.

00:03:03.383 --> 00:03:05.610
- And they mention Sandy Hook, don't they?

00:03:05.610 --> 00:03:06.443
- Yeah.

00:03:06.443 --> 00:03:08.310
- Plaintiffs move 130 into evidence.

00:03:08.310 --> 00:03:09.600
- All right, so this is the kind of stuff

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:10.590
you'd never see in movies.

00:03:10.590 --> 00:03:13.350
It's the meat and potatoes of litigation.

00:03:13.350 --> 00:03:14.610
It's kinda the boring stuff.

00:03:14.610 --> 00:03:15.660
You're laying a foundation

00:03:15.660 --> 00:03:18.180
that this document is
what it purports to be,

00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:19.680
this witness has verified it,

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:21.600
and then you're moving it into evidence.

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:24.390
But so far, seems pretty routine.

00:03:24.390 --> 00:03:26.520
We're about to find out
why that is not the case,

00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:29.670
and it is a brilliant act
of setting everyone up

00:03:29.670 --> 00:03:31.470
for a fall here.

00:03:31.470 --> 00:03:32.720
- [Judge] Any objections?

00:03:34.500 --> 00:03:36.180
- The judge asks, "Any objections?"

00:03:36.180 --> 00:03:37.980
No. Why would there be any objections?

00:03:37.980 --> 00:03:39.900
This is probably just one of the millions

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:41.610
of documents that were turned over.

00:03:41.610 --> 00:03:42.899
- No. No objections.

00:03:42.899 --> 00:03:44.670
- [Judge] Plaintiff's 130 is admitted.

00:03:44.670 --> 00:03:46.230
- No objection.

00:03:46.230 --> 00:03:49.980
So the opposing counsel
doesn't quite understand

00:03:49.980 --> 00:03:51.660
what this document is at this point,

00:03:51.660 --> 00:03:54.000
and so he waives any
objection he might make

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:56.790
to entering this particular
document into evidence.

00:03:56.790 --> 00:03:59.010
Now Jones has been screwed.

00:03:59.010 --> 00:04:02.160
- Let's zoom in on the little
article up in the corner.

00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:04.140
I'm gonna walk over to this
to you so I can kinda point.

00:04:04.140 --> 00:04:05.708
I know it's a little hard to read.

00:04:05.708 --> 00:04:07.590
You might be able to read it a
little better on your screen.

00:04:07.590 --> 00:04:09.060
But this is a zoom of...

00:04:09.060 --> 00:04:12.780
Mr. Watson has sent you a
screenshot from infowars.com.

00:04:12.780 --> 00:04:14.677
Correct?
- Appears to be.

00:04:14.677 --> 00:04:16.620
- Yeah, and it has an article here.

00:04:16.620 --> 00:04:18.030
Right?
- Yeah.

00:04:18.030 --> 00:04:21.690
- And it says "Staged?
Video Shows Hospital

00:04:21.690 --> 00:04:25.890
Using Dummies in ER for
Coronavirus Footage."

00:04:25.890 --> 00:04:26.760
- [Alex] I believe so.

00:04:26.760 --> 00:04:28.860
- [Mark] Let's go to the
first message from Mr. Watson.

00:04:28.860 --> 00:04:30.390
Read along with me, Mr. Jones.

00:04:30.390 --> 00:04:32.850
He says, "This is a video"-

00:04:32.850 --> 00:04:36.300
- Sorry, excuse me one second.
- No problem. Take your time.

00:04:36.300 --> 00:04:38.310
- Man, Jones has a nasty cough.

00:04:38.310 --> 00:04:40.140
I guess he needs to take
more of those supplements

00:04:40.140 --> 00:04:41.670
that he hawks on his channel.

00:04:41.670 --> 00:04:43.920
- You may not think it's a
problem, but it's a real one.

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:44.753
- [Mark] Sure.

00:04:44.753 --> 00:04:46.650
Mr. Watson says, "This is a video

00:04:46.650 --> 00:04:49.530
of a medical student training to intubate.

00:04:49.530 --> 00:04:51.750
Makes us look ridiculous,

00:04:51.750 --> 00:04:54.750
suggesting this means COVID is fake.

00:04:54.750 --> 00:04:57.537
Sandy Hook all over again."

00:04:58.895 --> 00:05:00.540
Did I read that correctly?
- Yes.

00:05:00.540 --> 00:05:02.250
- [Mark] This is the next message.

00:05:02.250 --> 00:05:03.600
What'd you tell Mr. Watson?

00:05:04.837 --> 00:05:06.090
- "I get it."

00:05:06.090 --> 00:05:06.923
- What has happened

00:05:06.923 --> 00:05:08.220
is this is something that has taken place

00:05:08.220 --> 00:05:11.730
after all of the Sandy
Hook-related communications.

00:05:11.730 --> 00:05:14.670
One of his employees says that
"we shouldn't publish this

00:05:14.670 --> 00:05:15.930
because it makes us look stupid

00:05:15.930 --> 00:05:18.420
and it's evocative of what happened

00:05:18.420 --> 00:05:20.280
during the Sandy Hook stuff,"

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:22.440
and Jones says, "I get it."

00:05:22.440 --> 00:05:24.630
Everything Jones has said up until now

00:05:24.630 --> 00:05:27.180
is basically a lie, and it also shows

00:05:27.180 --> 00:05:29.730
that Jones and his attorneys
never actually searched

00:05:29.730 --> 00:05:31.650
for responsive communications.

00:05:31.650 --> 00:05:34.590
So being caught in that particular lie

00:05:34.590 --> 00:05:37.380
and falsity is gonna be much, much worse

00:05:37.380 --> 00:05:39.600
than if they had just
turned over these documents

00:05:39.600 --> 00:05:40.440
in the first place,

00:05:40.440 --> 00:05:42.690
and that is why you comply with discovery.

00:05:42.690 --> 00:05:45.150
But let's see how the knife gets twisted.

00:05:45.150 --> 00:05:47.310
- [Mark] Mr. Jones, it's true

00:05:47.310 --> 00:05:51.900
that this article is right
now live on infowars.com.

00:05:51.900 --> 00:05:53.700
I can pull it up. Right?

00:05:53.700 --> 00:05:55.503
- I've never seen this text message.

00:05:56.430 --> 00:05:58.080
I guess you guys got Paul's.

00:05:58.080 --> 00:06:00.330
My phone didn't save 'em,
so that's fine with me.

00:06:00.330 --> 00:06:02.070
- [Mark] Your phone didn't
save this text message?

00:06:02.070 --> 00:06:04.440
- I told you guys, I
gave it to the lawyers,

00:06:04.440 --> 00:06:07.004
who said they drained the phone
and didn't find that stuff.

00:06:07.004 --> 00:06:08.350
I don't have my phone.

00:06:08.350 --> 00:06:09.780
- Yeah, and now he's trying

00:06:09.780 --> 00:06:11.497
to throw his lawyers under the bus.

00:06:11.497 --> 00:06:13.650
"That must not have been on my phone.

00:06:13.650 --> 00:06:14.850
I searched my phones,

00:06:14.850 --> 00:06:16.530
and I turned stuff over to the lawyers.

00:06:16.530 --> 00:06:18.930
If you didn't have this,
it's because of the lawyers."

00:06:18.930 --> 00:06:20.400
That's not gonna go as planned.

00:06:20.400 --> 00:06:22.140
- [Mark] You gave it to the lawyers?

00:06:22.140 --> 00:06:22.973
They were supposed to find it?

00:06:22.973 --> 00:06:24.240
So that's what your testimony is?

00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:25.691
- No, I searched it as well.

00:06:25.691 --> 00:06:27.818
I don't know who this one's taken from.

00:06:27.818 --> 00:06:29.220
I mean, I just, I turned the phone over

00:06:29.220 --> 00:06:30.529
and said to take the stuff off.

00:06:30.529 --> 00:06:32.220
- [Mark] Can I have you
look in the very bottom,

00:06:32.220 --> 00:06:34.773
below the very bottom left corner?

00:06:35.850 --> 00:06:38.453
Is that your phone number?
- Yes.

00:06:38.453 --> 00:06:40.912
So you did get my text messages.

00:06:40.912 --> 00:06:44.375
You said you didn't.
Nice trick. (stammers)

00:06:44.375 --> 00:06:47.103
- [Mark] (chortles)
Yes, Mr. Jones. Indeed.

00:06:48.510 --> 00:06:49.530
- The lawyer can't help himself

00:06:49.530 --> 00:06:51.630
because he knows what the
next question is going to be.

00:06:51.630 --> 00:06:56.460
Alex Jones is trying to
run this really fine line

00:06:56.460 --> 00:06:59.400
where yes, he held stuff back.

00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:00.660
He got sanctioned so badly

00:07:00.660 --> 00:07:02.910
that he lost the liability
phase of the trial.

00:07:02.910 --> 00:07:04.050
He thinks it's safe to say, "Well,

00:07:04.050 --> 00:07:06.300
I couldn't have possibly held it back

00:07:06.300 --> 00:07:08.880
if you have all of these text messages."

00:07:08.880 --> 00:07:10.410
But now the final phase

00:07:10.410 --> 00:07:12.600
of the trap is just about to be sprung.

00:07:12.600 --> 00:07:14.031
- [Mark] You didn't give
this text message to me.

00:07:14.031 --> 00:07:15.330
You don't know where this came from.

00:07:15.330 --> 00:07:17.520
Do you know where I got this?
- No.

00:07:17.520 --> 00:07:20.700
- 12 days ago, your attorneys messed up

00:07:20.700 --> 00:07:23.790
and sent me an entire digital copy

00:07:23.790 --> 00:07:25.800
of your entire cell phone

00:07:25.800 --> 00:07:28.230
with every text message you've sent

00:07:28.230 --> 00:07:31.320
for the past two years and, when informed,

00:07:31.320 --> 00:07:34.290
did not take any steps to
identify it as privileged

00:07:34.290 --> 00:07:37.380
or protected in any way,
and as of two days ago,

00:07:37.380 --> 00:07:39.480
it fell free and clear into my possession,

00:07:39.480 --> 00:07:42.480
and that is how I know you
lied to me when you said

00:07:42.480 --> 00:07:44.400
you didn't have text
messages about Sandy Hook.

00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:45.719
Did you know that?

00:07:45.719 --> 00:07:47.340
- I told you the truth.

00:07:47.340 --> 00:07:48.750
This is your Perry Mason moment.

00:07:48.750 --> 00:07:51.510
I gave them my phone and then-

00:07:51.510 --> 00:07:53.278
- [Judge] Mr. Jones, you
need to answer the question.

00:07:53.278 --> 00:07:55.109
- No, I-
- Did you know this happened?

00:07:55.109 --> 00:07:57.840
- So normally, there's
some professional courtesy

00:07:57.840 --> 00:07:59.760
amongst lawyers where you're not going

00:07:59.760 --> 00:08:01.560
to just absolutely destroy them

00:08:01.560 --> 00:08:02.580
and throw them under the bus.

00:08:02.580 --> 00:08:05.640
Here, the lawyer is throwing Jones

00:08:05.640 --> 00:08:07.410
and his attorneys under the bus.

00:08:07.410 --> 00:08:10.770
I've never heard of
something this inflammatory

00:08:10.770 --> 00:08:13.380
coming out against
another opposing attorney

00:08:13.380 --> 00:08:15.570
during the course of a trial.

00:08:15.570 --> 00:08:18.930
The lawyer screwed up
and sent an entire copy

00:08:18.930 --> 00:08:22.200
of Jones's phone to opposing counsel

00:08:22.200 --> 00:08:24.090
and then didn't claw it back.

00:08:24.090 --> 00:08:25.410
There are a million things that you can do

00:08:25.410 --> 00:08:26.340
to claw that back.

00:08:26.340 --> 00:08:27.720
The lawyer didn't do that.

00:08:27.720 --> 00:08:30.420
As a result, Alex Jones is on the stand

00:08:30.420 --> 00:08:32.340
just getting destroyed,

00:08:32.340 --> 00:08:35.220
being shown that everything
he's talked about is a lie,

00:08:35.220 --> 00:08:37.486
that they tried to continue

00:08:37.486 --> 00:08:40.826
to hide information from the plaintiffs,

00:08:40.826 --> 00:08:43.410
and it's only because of
this inadvertent disclosure

00:08:43.410 --> 00:08:45.030
that they finally got the documents

00:08:45.030 --> 00:08:47.520
that they had been entitled
to this entire time.

00:08:47.520 --> 00:08:48.960
The jury is hearing all of this,

00:08:48.960 --> 00:08:52.050
and they're finding out how much of a liar

00:08:52.050 --> 00:08:54.090
and a fabulist Alex Jones is,

00:08:54.090 --> 00:08:55.740
if they didn't know already,

00:08:55.740 --> 00:08:58.860
but also that there are
shenanigans as well.

00:08:58.860 --> 00:09:01.830
And during the course of trial,

00:09:01.830 --> 00:09:03.540
not only the witness's credibility

00:09:03.540 --> 00:09:05.520
but the lawyer's
credibility is everything,

00:09:05.520 --> 00:09:07.290
and once you lose that credibility,

00:09:07.290 --> 00:09:08.520
you're probably screwed,

00:09:08.520 --> 00:09:10.710
because the jury will find ways

00:09:10.710 --> 00:09:13.200
to award people that they believe

00:09:13.200 --> 00:09:15.630
and punish people who they don't believe.

00:09:15.630 --> 00:09:18.600
Oh, and by the way, it's
of course impossible

00:09:18.600 --> 00:09:20.730
to prep a witness like Alex Jones

00:09:20.730 --> 00:09:25.230
to be an effective advocate
on the stand, but man,

00:09:25.230 --> 00:09:28.680
you gotta know just the
basics of self-preservation.

00:09:28.680 --> 00:09:30.810
Don't tell the cross-examining attorney

00:09:30.810 --> 00:09:33.030
that this is his Perry Mason moment.

00:09:33.030 --> 00:09:37.020
It kinda signals to the jury
that you're getting screwed

00:09:37.020 --> 00:09:39.420
and you deserve to be screwed.

00:09:39.420 --> 00:09:40.860
- No, I don't know how
this happened, but I mean,

00:09:40.860 --> 00:09:43.304
I told you I gave him my phone
over and you just said no.

00:09:43.304 --> 00:09:44.720
- Just answer the question.

00:09:44.720 --> 00:09:47.460
- [Mark] You said in your deposition

00:09:47.460 --> 00:09:48.990
you searched your phone.

00:09:48.990 --> 00:09:51.060
You said you pulled down the texts

00:09:51.060 --> 00:09:53.820
and did the search
function for Sandy Hook.

00:09:53.820 --> 00:09:56.250
- I have several different
phones with this number,

00:09:56.250 --> 00:09:57.736
but I did, yeah.

00:09:57.736 --> 00:09:58.569
Of course I...

00:09:58.569 --> 00:10:00.060
I mean, that's why you got it.

00:10:00.060 --> 00:10:01.020
- There's all kinds of things

00:10:01.020 --> 00:10:03.060
that can go wrong in discovery,

00:10:03.060 --> 00:10:05.670
but the easiest, the
absolute easiest thing

00:10:05.670 --> 00:10:07.200
that you can do as a client

00:10:07.200 --> 00:10:09.840
or as a lawyer is to do a word search,

00:10:09.840 --> 00:10:12.510
and when the entire trial revolves

00:10:12.510 --> 00:10:15.450
around the phrase Sandy Hook,

00:10:15.450 --> 00:10:17.504
the absolute bare minimum,

00:10:17.504 --> 00:10:20.100
the necessary thing that you have to do,

00:10:20.100 --> 00:10:22.650
is do a word search for Sandy Hook.

00:10:22.650 --> 00:10:23.483
And it's very clear

00:10:23.483 --> 00:10:26.220
that if they had done
this absolute minimum,

00:10:26.220 --> 00:10:29.790
fundamental thing, this
text would've been found

00:10:29.790 --> 00:10:31.470
and it would've been turned
over to the other side.

00:10:31.470 --> 00:10:34.020
But they clearly didn't do that.

00:10:34.020 --> 00:10:36.660
- [Mark] No, Mr. Jones,
that's not what happened.

00:10:36.660 --> 00:10:40.140
- My lawyer sent it to you,
but I'm hiding it. Okay.

00:10:40.140 --> 00:10:44.550
- Mr. Jones, please just answer questions.

00:10:44.550 --> 00:10:46.170
There's no question.

00:10:46.170 --> 00:10:48.990
Mr. Bankston, also, only ask questions.

00:10:48.990 --> 00:10:49.823
- [Mark] Sure.

00:10:49.823 --> 00:10:51.290
- And a little bit of foreshadowing here,

00:10:52.380 --> 00:10:55.200
this is really underselling what happened

00:10:55.200 --> 00:10:56.340
between the two lawyers.

00:10:56.340 --> 00:10:58.260
That the opposing counsel,

00:10:58.260 --> 00:11:02.250
the defense attorney,
Reynal, sent the full copy

00:11:02.250 --> 00:11:04.440
of Jones's phone is just
the tip of the iceberg.

00:11:04.440 --> 00:11:07.800
He actually sent way, way more than that

00:11:07.800 --> 00:11:09.900
to opposing counsel, effectively probably

00:11:09.900 --> 00:11:13.650
the entire case file from the
defense to the plaintiffs.

00:11:13.650 --> 00:11:16.650
Just absolute malpractice,
and we're gonna get to that,

00:11:16.650 --> 00:11:18.540
because that is, if you can believe it,

00:11:18.540 --> 00:11:23.340
even juicier than this
cross-examination is indicating.

00:11:23.340 --> 00:11:26.587
- [Mark] Mr. Jones, in
discovery, you were asked,

00:11:26.587 --> 00:11:29.787
"Do you have Sandy Hook text
messages on your phone?"

00:11:29.787 --> 00:11:31.746
And you said no, correct?

00:11:31.746 --> 00:11:33.480
- I mean, if I was
mistaken, I was mistaken,

00:11:33.480 --> 00:11:35.490
but you've got the messages right there.

00:11:35.490 --> 00:11:37.573
- [Mark] You know what perjury is, right?

00:11:37.573 --> 00:11:39.292
I just wanna make sure you
know before we go any further.

00:11:39.292 --> 00:11:41.193
Do you know what it is?

00:11:41.193 --> 00:11:42.026
- Yes, I do.

00:11:42.026 --> 00:11:42.990
I mean, I'm not a tech guy.

00:11:42.990 --> 00:11:45.480
I told you I gave, in my testimony,

00:11:45.480 --> 00:11:47.850
the phone to the lawyers
before or whatever,

00:11:47.850 --> 00:11:50.940
and so you got my phone, but
we didn't give it to you.

00:11:50.940 --> 00:11:51.900
- [Mark] Please remember, if you need

00:11:51.900 --> 00:11:53.392
to assert the Fifth Amendment, you can.

00:11:53.392 --> 00:11:54.225
I need you to know you can do that.

00:11:54.225 --> 00:11:55.290
- Ouch. Ouch.

00:11:55.290 --> 00:11:58.530
- [Mark] But you testified
under oath previously

00:11:58.530 --> 00:12:02.190
that you personally searched your phone

00:12:02.190 --> 00:12:05.280
for the phrase Sandy Hook
and there were no messages.

00:12:05.280 --> 00:12:07.073
You said that under oath.
- Yes.

00:12:07.073 --> 00:12:08.400
- [Mark] And you lied when you said it.

00:12:08.400 --> 00:12:09.840
- No, I did not lie.

00:12:09.840 --> 00:12:11.070
- And here, the plaintiffs' attorney

00:12:11.070 --> 00:12:12.900
is kind of gilding the lily a little bit.

00:12:12.900 --> 00:12:16.560
He has now absolutely
established that Jones said

00:12:16.560 --> 00:12:19.920
he did a search for the words Sandy Hook

00:12:19.920 --> 00:12:21.990
and that he previously testified

00:12:21.990 --> 00:12:24.990
that he did not have any
of those communications,

00:12:24.990 --> 00:12:27.300
which we now know is
absolutely not the case.

00:12:27.300 --> 00:12:30.330
Jones tries to backpedal by
saying, "Look, I was mistaken.

00:12:30.330 --> 00:12:34.950
I had many phones, but I thought
that was true at the time."

00:12:34.950 --> 00:12:37.200
But that seems extraordinarily unlikely

00:12:37.200 --> 00:12:39.480
under the cross-examination
that we just saw,

00:12:39.480 --> 00:12:40.313
because, again, you're trying

00:12:40.313 --> 00:12:42.150
to impugn the witness's character

00:12:42.150 --> 00:12:44.580
and show that the witness is a liar.

00:12:44.580 --> 00:12:46.980
So as Mr. Bankston has done here,

00:12:46.980 --> 00:12:49.290
you teed up with a prior
inconsistent statement

00:12:49.290 --> 00:12:51.660
and then show them either the evidence

00:12:51.660 --> 00:12:54.120
or the prior statement
that is contradictory

00:12:54.120 --> 00:12:56.190
to what they previously testified to.

00:12:56.190 --> 00:12:58.320
So when I saw this clip,
I was flabbergasted.

00:12:58.320 --> 00:13:01.080
How in the world did this happen?

00:13:01.080 --> 00:13:04.500
How did these documents
get turned over after years

00:13:04.500 --> 00:13:08.040
of being hidden by Alex
Jones and his attorneys?

00:13:08.040 --> 00:13:10.890
Well, luckily, the court
held a full hearing

00:13:10.890 --> 00:13:13.170
on this matter to discuss.

00:13:13.170 --> 00:13:16.590
Jones's counsel finally
moved for a mistrial

00:13:16.590 --> 00:13:19.440
the day after this testimony came out.

00:13:19.440 --> 00:13:21.840
Now, this hearing did not go well.

00:13:21.840 --> 00:13:23.310
So let's dive into this hearing,

00:13:23.310 --> 00:13:25.200
because from a seasoned litigator,

00:13:25.200 --> 00:13:27.360
I think this is actually
even more interesting

00:13:27.360 --> 00:13:29.760
than the original cross-examination.

00:13:29.760 --> 00:13:31.860
So Jones's attorney is
asking for a mistrial.

00:13:31.860 --> 00:13:35.010
Effectively, he's saying
that the malfeasance

00:13:35.010 --> 00:13:37.080
from the other side is
so terrible that we need

00:13:37.080 --> 00:13:39.300
to end this trial and do it over again.

00:13:39.300 --> 00:13:41.550
So how in the world did this happen?

00:13:41.550 --> 00:13:44.010
Well, let's hear it
straight from Andino Reynal,

00:13:44.010 --> 00:13:45.840
the defense attorney's, mouth.

00:13:45.840 --> 00:13:47.250
- A couple weeks before trial,

00:13:47.250 --> 00:13:52.080
a file transfer link was sent
to the plaintiffs' attorneys,

00:13:52.080 --> 00:13:54.240
specifically Mr. Bankston.

00:13:54.240 --> 00:13:57.360
That link linked up a lot of files

00:13:57.360 --> 00:13:59.130
that shouldn't have been connected to it.

00:13:59.130 --> 00:14:00.960
- Okay, so he's saying
that a link was sent,

00:14:00.960 --> 00:14:03.720
probably a Dropbox link,
containing some documents

00:14:03.720 --> 00:14:06.000
that needed to be turned
over to the plaintiffs.

00:14:06.000 --> 00:14:07.500
Apparently that link contained much,

00:14:07.500 --> 00:14:09.090
much more than was intended.

00:14:09.090 --> 00:14:11.490
- He said, "I take it
this was sent in error,"

00:14:11.490 --> 00:14:13.447
and I responded to him and I said,

00:14:13.447 --> 00:14:15.390
"It has been sent in error.

00:14:15.390 --> 00:14:16.800
Please disregard the link.

00:14:16.800 --> 00:14:18.930
I will work on preparing a new one."

00:14:18.930 --> 00:14:20.700
- Okay, so we hear from
the defense attorney

00:14:20.700 --> 00:14:22.980
that the plaintiffs'
attorney did notify him

00:14:22.980 --> 00:14:25.200
that the production had been made

00:14:25.200 --> 00:14:26.910
and it was probably in error.

00:14:26.910 --> 00:14:30.090
That's super bad news
for the defense attorney

00:14:30.090 --> 00:14:31.770
because if you've been notified

00:14:31.770 --> 00:14:34.350
and you don't do anything to claw it back,

00:14:34.350 --> 00:14:36.030
well, then you're probably screwed,

00:14:36.030 --> 00:14:38.490
which, spoiler, is what happens here.

00:14:38.490 --> 00:14:41.519
- I mean, we have a
situation here which is akin

00:14:41.519 --> 00:14:45.360
to me mistakenly giving
him the key to a room,

00:14:45.360 --> 00:14:47.550
and, you know, he opens
the door to the room,

00:14:47.550 --> 00:14:49.440
and instead of finding
what he expected to find,

00:14:49.440 --> 00:14:50.820
he sees other doors.

00:14:50.820 --> 00:14:52.717
And he recognized that and told me,

00:14:52.717 --> 00:14:55.020
"I don't think you meant
to give me this key."

00:14:55.020 --> 00:14:57.000
And I said, "Please throw it away."

00:14:57.000 --> 00:14:59.223
Apparently, at some point thereafter,

00:15:00.570 --> 00:15:01.403
he said, "You know what?

00:15:01.403 --> 00:15:03.930
I'd kinda really like to see
what's behind those doors."

00:15:03.930 --> 00:15:05.010
- This is an interesting argument,

00:15:05.010 --> 00:15:06.450
and he's doing probably a good job

00:15:06.450 --> 00:15:08.130
of trying to paint the plaintiffs' actions

00:15:08.130 --> 00:15:09.570
in the worst possible light,

00:15:09.570 --> 00:15:10.770
because that's what you're gonna need

00:15:10.770 --> 00:15:14.400
to convince the judge to claw
back all of these documents.

00:15:14.400 --> 00:15:16.950
He's saying that the plaintiffs' attorney

00:15:16.950 --> 00:15:20.160
abused the inadvertent production

00:15:20.160 --> 00:15:22.680
when rummaging around in
all of these documents

00:15:22.680 --> 00:15:25.230
and found what was good for his case

00:15:25.230 --> 00:15:28.650
and I guess didn't notify
or destroy the documents

00:15:28.650 --> 00:15:30.660
once they were in his possession.

00:15:30.660 --> 00:15:33.600
- And that's why we're
here seeking protection

00:15:33.600 --> 00:15:35.970
under the Texas Rules of Civil Procedure

00:15:35.970 --> 00:15:39.410
as well as the court's
confidentiality order.

00:15:39.410 --> 00:15:40.350
- So here's the relief

00:15:40.350 --> 00:15:41.490
that the defense attorney is looking for.

00:15:41.490 --> 00:15:42.990
He's asking for a protective order

00:15:42.990 --> 00:15:45.150
to stop disseminating the documents,

00:15:45.150 --> 00:15:46.710
to claw them back, to assert privilege,

00:15:46.710 --> 00:15:48.810
and to destroy those documents

00:15:48.810 --> 00:15:51.330
that have been inadvertently
turned over to the plaintiffs.

00:15:51.330 --> 00:15:52.163
- You're right.

00:15:52.163 --> 00:15:53.790
I'm just gonna start
unsending these one by one.

00:15:53.790 --> 00:15:54.720
- You wrote, "Unsend."

00:15:54.720 --> 00:15:56.218
You keep sending the word unsend.

00:15:56.218 --> 00:15:57.750
"Unsend, unsend, unsend.

00:15:57.750 --> 00:15:58.583
Please do not read.

00:15:58.583 --> 00:15:59.760
I'm begging you."

00:15:59.760 --> 00:16:02.610
- I hate to be put into this position

00:16:02.610 --> 00:16:06.390
by the conduct of plaintiffs' counsel.

00:16:06.390 --> 00:16:08.850
- And of course he is trying
to blame plaintiffs' attorney

00:16:08.850 --> 00:16:10.890
for this mess, not he himself,

00:16:10.890 --> 00:16:13.620
the one that inadvertently
produced all this stuff.

00:16:13.620 --> 00:16:16.170
- And that's why, in
addition to the request

00:16:16.170 --> 00:16:19.980
that they delete the
information from their servers

00:16:19.980 --> 00:16:23.490
and delete the link, we're also requesting

00:16:23.490 --> 00:16:25.110
at this point, unfortunately, a mistrial.

00:16:25.110 --> 00:16:26.730
- And now we hear from
the plaintiffs' attorney

00:16:26.730 --> 00:16:27.900
and hear his side of it.

00:16:27.900 --> 00:16:30.700
- First, let me just go through
the facts of their side.

00:16:32.419 --> 00:16:35.632
This link of materials was sent to me.

00:16:35.632 --> 00:16:37.259
It was sent to Mr. Ogden.

00:16:37.259 --> 00:16:40.230
It was sent by Mr.
Reynal's legal assistant.

00:16:40.230 --> 00:16:42.420
- Ah, okay, so this link was sent

00:16:42.420 --> 00:16:45.780
not by defense attorney Andino Reynal

00:16:45.780 --> 00:16:48.690
but actually by his legal assistant.

00:16:48.690 --> 00:16:51.960
I think that legal assistant
has probably been fired.

00:16:51.960 --> 00:16:53.400
An easy mistake to make,

00:16:53.400 --> 00:16:56.730
but it's a mistake that
you absolutely cannot make.

00:16:56.730 --> 00:16:58.740
- As I informed Mr. Reynal,

00:16:58.740 --> 00:17:00.780
we started downloading those materials.

00:17:00.780 --> 00:17:01.980
I was busy on trial prep.

00:17:01.980 --> 00:17:04.290
I told my paralegal,
"Get these on our server.

00:17:04.290 --> 00:17:05.730
Download everything now." All right?

00:17:05.730 --> 00:17:09.660
A little bit after he gets
this stuff downloading,

00:17:09.660 --> 00:17:10.493
he's like, "Wait a second.

00:17:10.493 --> 00:17:12.060
This file that we now have on our server

00:17:12.060 --> 00:17:14.255
has clogged up our entire server.

00:17:14.255 --> 00:17:15.990
It's, like, 300 gigs.

00:17:15.990 --> 00:17:16.860
What's going on?"

00:17:16.860 --> 00:17:17.820
- Okay, so the plaintiffs' lawyer

00:17:17.820 --> 00:17:19.260
does what probably anyone would do.

00:17:19.260 --> 00:17:21.420
You get a link, and you
start downloading it.

00:17:21.420 --> 00:17:23.670
And then only once you've
started downloading it

00:17:23.670 --> 00:17:27.750
do you realize that it's a
huge production, 300 gigabytes,

00:17:27.750 --> 00:17:30.840
and you probably don't
know what's in that link

00:17:30.840 --> 00:17:32.250
until you've downloaded everything,

00:17:32.250 --> 00:17:34.980
but it's so big that it's
actually clogging his servers,

00:17:34.980 --> 00:17:36.870
which is hilarious.

00:17:36.870 --> 00:17:39.000
Well, not for Jones's attorneys, though.

00:17:39.000 --> 00:17:40.637
- Mr. Reynal actually (indistinct),

00:17:43.326 --> 00:17:45.244
so I know he knows how to read it,

00:17:45.244 --> 00:17:46.620
but he still hasn't done it.

00:17:46.620 --> 00:17:50.130
At the moment, he is up
here requesting a mistrial

00:17:50.130 --> 00:17:51.690
because he hasn't followed the rule.

00:17:51.690 --> 00:17:54.480
Even today, (indistinct)
has not followed the rule.

00:17:54.480 --> 00:17:57.450
The rule requires that production is...

00:17:57.450 --> 00:17:58.650
Any privilege is waived.

00:17:58.650 --> 00:17:59.790
- Okay, this is easy to miss,

00:17:59.790 --> 00:18:02.130
but this is the most important part.

00:18:02.130 --> 00:18:04.080
The plaintiffs' attorney
is referring to briefing.

00:18:04.080 --> 00:18:07.050
He's saying that the defense's
brief is citing a part

00:18:07.050 --> 00:18:08.940
of the Texas Rule of Civil Procedure

00:18:08.940 --> 00:18:11.257
about inadvertent
disclosure, and he's saying,

00:18:11.257 --> 00:18:13.260
"I know that opposing counsel,

00:18:13.260 --> 00:18:14.970
the defense attorney, is able to read,

00:18:14.970 --> 00:18:17.430
because he is citing to the operative part

00:18:17.430 --> 00:18:19.650
of the Texas Rule of Civil Procedure,

00:18:19.650 --> 00:18:22.050
but it actually is
very, very bad for him,"

00:18:22.050 --> 00:18:24.390
and he talks about the actual standard.

00:18:24.390 --> 00:18:27.030
What happens when you make
an inadvertent disclosure?

00:18:27.030 --> 00:18:28.230
Lawyers write these rules,

00:18:28.230 --> 00:18:30.480
so obviously they're gonna make it

00:18:30.480 --> 00:18:32.490
so that if you inadvertently
disclose something,

00:18:32.490 --> 00:18:34.050
there's a way to get it back.

00:18:34.050 --> 00:18:38.280
- Any privilege is
waived if within 10 days,

00:18:38.280 --> 00:18:41.430
or if you order a shorter
time, but within 10 days,

00:18:41.430 --> 00:18:43.830
the producing party of when
they actually discover,

00:18:43.830 --> 00:18:45.843
so 10 days from the moment I told him,

00:18:47.010 --> 00:18:50.310
that they identify
specifically the material

00:18:50.310 --> 00:18:54.153
or information produced and
state the privilege asserted.

00:18:55.260 --> 00:18:57.420
I have not heard any privilege.

00:18:57.420 --> 00:18:59.780
I have never heard a privilege
asserted over any document.

00:18:59.780 --> 00:19:01.770
- So the plaintiffs' lawyer points out

00:19:01.770 --> 00:19:05.250
that he notified opposing
counsel of the disclosure.

00:19:05.250 --> 00:19:07.500
Opposing counsel didn't do anything.

00:19:07.500 --> 00:19:09.390
He didn't supplement his production.

00:19:09.390 --> 00:19:11.040
He never asserted privilege.

00:19:11.040 --> 00:19:12.840
That means all of these documents,

00:19:12.840 --> 00:19:16.560
all 300 gigabytes of
documents, are fair game,

00:19:16.560 --> 00:19:19.110
and the plaintiffs'
lawyer is probably right.

00:19:19.110 --> 00:19:20.220
- But even more important than that

00:19:20.220 --> 00:19:21.360
is all of the stuff in there,

00:19:21.360 --> 00:19:23.580
he cannot now come into court and claim

00:19:23.580 --> 00:19:26.550
2.3 gigabytes of phone material

00:19:26.550 --> 00:19:28.860
blanket designation of confidentiality.

00:19:28.860 --> 00:19:30.840
The protective order prohibits that.

00:19:30.840 --> 00:19:33.690
You must identify specifically
the documents that are,

00:19:33.690 --> 00:19:37.680
and things like Mr. Jones
and his intimate messages

00:19:37.680 --> 00:19:40.020
with Roger Stone are not confidential.

00:19:40.020 --> 00:19:41.280
- And then the plaintiffs' lawyer explains

00:19:41.280 --> 00:19:43.350
that the defense not only failed

00:19:43.350 --> 00:19:46.380
to uphold their obligations under 193.3,

00:19:46.380 --> 00:19:48.492
but they didn't comply with
the court's protective order.

00:19:48.492 --> 00:19:51.750
The court can put in additional rules

00:19:51.750 --> 00:19:54.300
that govern the disclosure of information.

00:19:54.300 --> 00:19:55.980
They didn't follow that.

00:19:55.980 --> 00:19:57.600
They didn't supplement their production,

00:19:57.600 --> 00:20:00.660
so even the email that says
"Please disregard that link,

00:20:00.660 --> 00:20:01.800
I'll send you another link,"

00:20:01.800 --> 00:20:03.660
he didn't even follow through with that.

00:20:03.660 --> 00:20:06.450
And oh, by the way, the vast majority

00:20:06.450 --> 00:20:08.790
of these documents are not privileged

00:20:08.790 --> 00:20:12.270
and should've been disclosed
years and years ago.

00:20:12.270 --> 00:20:14.700
- So I don't think that you have complied

00:20:14.700 --> 00:20:16.560
with the protective order.

00:20:16.560 --> 00:20:20.700
I will let you do so now

00:20:20.700 --> 00:20:22.860
if there are documents and you would like

00:20:22.860 --> 00:20:24.780
to mark them as confidential,

00:20:24.780 --> 00:20:26.760
and we can go through the process

00:20:26.760 --> 00:20:29.430
and I can review them and make a decision,

00:20:29.430 --> 00:20:33.240
but I'm not going to
seal the entire quantity

00:20:33.240 --> 00:20:35.790
of information without
knowing what's in it

00:20:35.790 --> 00:20:38.220
when the rules weren't followed properly.

00:20:38.220 --> 00:20:40.878
- It's a tremendous amount
of information, Your Honor.

00:20:40.878 --> 00:20:41.711
- I know.

00:20:41.711 --> 00:20:45.210
The problem is it probably
should've been disclosed

00:20:45.210 --> 00:20:48.180
a year ago or longer, and then
there would've been plenty

00:20:48.180 --> 00:20:49.680
of time to look through all of it.

00:20:49.680 --> 00:20:52.680
- And in the end, the judge
doesn't seal these documents.

00:20:52.680 --> 00:20:55.710
She allows the defense to
try to assert privilege.

00:20:55.710 --> 00:20:57.360
She recognizes that the vast majority

00:20:57.360 --> 00:20:59.130
of these documents are not privileged

00:20:59.130 --> 00:21:00.840
and should've been turned over.

00:21:00.840 --> 00:21:03.120
And then, of course, the
defense's prior discovery abuses

00:21:03.120 --> 00:21:04.530
come back to bite them again

00:21:04.530 --> 00:21:06.390
because how in the world are they going

00:21:06.390 --> 00:21:10.281
to actually assert privilege
or review 300 gigabytes worth

00:21:10.281 --> 00:21:13.980
of files in a couple
of days while at trial?

00:21:13.980 --> 00:21:16.230
But the judge doesn't have any sympathy.

00:21:16.230 --> 00:21:18.690
She says that this stuff
should've been turned over

00:21:18.690 --> 00:21:20.340
and the reason that
they're in this position

00:21:20.340 --> 00:21:21.900
is because of their own malfeasance.

00:21:21.900 --> 00:21:23.520
And the lesson here that clients learn

00:21:23.520 --> 00:21:26.910
time and time again is
